<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_displacement-current" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Displacement Current</title>







	<definition xml:id="def_maxwell-ampere">
		<title>Maxwell-Ampère Law</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>The <term>Ampère-Maxwell Law</term> combines Ampère's Law with Maxwell's correction to relate the magnetic field around a closed curve to both the current and the changing electric flux passing through the region bounded by the curve: 
			<md>\oint_S \vec{B}\cdot d\vec{l} = \mu_0 I_{enclosed} + \mu_0 \epsilon_0 \frac{\partial \Phi_E}{\partial t}</md></p>
			<p>
				This is a line integral, like the integral for <xref ref="e_to_v"/>. The circle over the integral symbol indicates that this is an integral over a <em>closed path</em>: when this is true, the line integral is known as the <term>circulation</term> of the magnetic field.
			</p>
			<p>
				The Ampère-Maxwell Law governs how both <em>currents</em> (made of moving electric charges) and <em>changing electric fields</em> create magnetic fields.  All other rules about the creation of magnetic fields, such as the <xref ref="def_mag-current"/>, ultimately come from this Law.  Magnetic fields always circulate around the current and the changing electric field, as shown in the <xref ref="x_fig_mag-full"/>.
			</p>
		</statement>
	</definition>


	
	
<!--NO AI-->
</section>

